{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_<scrubbed>","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019UvEBS87aq6Bao37yaP4Lc","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Domain research for session token storage","subagent_type":"speckit-pro:domain-researcher","prompt":"You are resolving a clarify question from the perspective of industry best practices and official documentation.\n\nQUESTION (security-tagged):\nQ1: Should session tokens be stored in HttpOnly cookies or in localStorage with a 15-minute TTL?\n\nYOUR TASK:\nResearch authoritative sources on session token storage. Look for:\n- OWASP guidance (Session Management Cheat Sheet, ASVS, Top 10)\n- Auth0 / Okta / Microsoft Identity / Google Identity recommendations\n- IETF / RFC guidance on token handling (RFC 6750, RFC 8725 for JWT BCP)\n- Framework-specific official docs (e.g., Next.js, Express, Django) for both approaches\n- Recent CVE patterns and XSS-vs-CSRF tradeoffs\n- Community consensus among reputable security engineers\n\nUse tavily_search, tavily_extract, context7, WebSearch, and WebFetch to gather evidence. Cite specific sources with URLs.\n\nReturn a structured answer:\n1. **Recommendation**: HttpOnly cookies OR localStorage+TTL OR \"context-dependent\" (with the determining factor named)\n2. **Evidence**: source URLs and direct quotes/paraphrases\n3. **Confidence**: high / medium / low\n4. **Tradeoffs**: the specific XSS-vs-CSRF tradeoff and any modern mitigations (SameSite=Strict/Lax, BFF pattern, token-bound cookies, etc.)\n\nDo NOT synthesize across other analysts' views. Do NOT write files. Return your structured answer only."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":"<scrubbed-usage>","diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<scrubbed-session>","uuid":"<scrubbed-uuid>"}
